# Email-Allowlist Dual-Gate Dashboard

## When to use

A small set of users (founders, finance leads, board members) need to see a dashboard that ordinary staff must not. Role-based permissions are too coarse — the role exists but you want only specific individuals within that role. Examples: CEO-only dashboard, CFO payment-approval view, on-call escalation panel.

You want defense **at the route, the sidebar, and the login redirect** so:

- Direct URL access redirects unauthorized users.
- The sidebar entry doesn't even appear for unauthorized users (no "what's that link?" curiosity).
- Authorized users land on the dashboard immediately after login, not on the generic homepage.

## The three gates

### Gate 1 — Post-login redirect

Wherever your app routes users after login (a `RoleRedirect` component, a `getDefaultRoute(user)` helper, etc.), check the allowlist first:

```ts
const PRIVILEGED_EMAILS = [
  'ceo@example.com',
  'cfo@example.com',
] as const;

function getDefaultRoute(user: { role: string; email?: string }): string {
  if (user.email && PRIVILEGED_EMAILS.includes(user.email.toLowerCase() as any)) {
    return '/exec-dashboard';
  }
  // fall through to role-based default
  switch (user.role) { /* ... */ }
}
```

### Gate 2 — Route component

The route's component should redirect unauthorized users away, even if they hit the URL directly:

```tsx
const ExecRoute = () => {
  const { user } = useAuth();
  const navigate = useNavigate();

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!user || !PRIVILEGED_EMAILS.includes(user.email?.toLowerCase() as any)) {
      navigate('/dashboard', { replace: true });
    }
  }, [user, navigate]);

  // Render nothing during the redirect frame.
  if (!user || !PRIVILEGED_EMAILS.includes(user.email?.toLowerCase() as any)) return null;

  return <Suspense fallback={<Loader />}><ExecDashboard /></Suspense>;
};

<Route path="/exec-dashboard" element={<ExecRoute />} />
```

### Gate 3 — Sidebar visibility

The sidebar filter hides the entry for non-allowed emails:

```ts
// useMenuItems.ts
return menuItems.filter(item => {
  if (item.title === 'Exec Dashboard') {
    const ok = PRIVILEGED_EMAILS.includes(user?.email?.toLowerCase() as any);
    if (!ok) return false;
  }
  // …other rules
  return true;
});
```

## Why three gates

| Without Gate | Failure mode |
|--------------|--------------|
| 1 (login redirect) | Allowed user lands on generic dashboard — they have to *find* their special page. Bad UX. |
| 2 (route component) | Anyone who guesses the URL gets in. Sidebar gate alone is **not** access control. |
| 3 (sidebar) | Unauthorized users see the link, get curious, try to access, find it blocked. Embarrassing. |

The route gate (#2) is the **only one that's actual access control** — #1 and #3 are UX. Server-side authorization on the API the dashboard calls is also required (RLS / row-level checks); these three gates are UI plumbing, not security.

## Shared constant — DRY at the source

Define `PRIVILEGED_EMAILS` once and import everywhere:

```ts
// src/lib/access.ts
export const PRIVILEGED_EMAILS = [
  'ceo@example.com',
  'cfo@example.com',
] as const;

export function isPrivileged(email?: string | null): boolean {
  if (!email) return false;
  return PRIVILEGED_EMAILS.includes(email.toLowerCase() as any);
}
```

Add an email by editing one file. All three gates pick it up.

## Example Domains

| Domain | Privileged dashboard |
|--------|---------------------|
| Finance | CFO payment-approval queue |
| Engineering | On-call incident dashboard |
| Sales | VP-sales pipeline forecast |
| HR | Compensation review board |
| Compliance | DPO audit panel |
| Hospital | Director's payment-deadlines view |

## Pitfalls

- **Case sensitivity** — always `.toLowerCase()` both sides. Email-allowlist bugs that "work in dev but not prod" are almost always case mismatch from a different SSO provider.
- **OAuth provider trims** — some providers (Google) lowercase emails; others (Microsoft) preserve case. Defensive lowercase is the right call.
- **Aliased emails** — `ceo+work@example.com` and `ceo@example.com` are the same Gmail account but different strings. Decide whether to normalize.
- **Group inboxes** — if the allowlist contains `finance-team@example.com` and multiple people share the mailbox, you've effectively bypassed authentication. Use individual emails.
- **Server-side enforcement is non-negotiable** — every API endpoint serving this dashboard must independently check the email or role. The three UI gates protect against *accidental* exposure, not malicious actors.
- **Mid-session role/email changes** — if a user's email changes mid-session (rare but possible with SSO), the cached `user` object is stale. Re-fetch on critical actions.
- **Test users** — keep `test+ceo@example.com` in the allowlist for staging but never let it slip into prod. CI lint to forbid `+test` in production allowlists.

